<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<graphml xmlns="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns/1.0/graphml.xsd">
  <key id="d2" for="node" attr.name="source_id" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d1" for="node" attr.name="description" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d0" for="node" attr.name="entity_type" attr.type="string" />
  <graph edgedefault="undirected">
    <node id="&quot;GRIST MILL BRIDGE, DAM AND MILL SITE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Grist Mill Bridge, Dam and Mill Site is a historic site consisting of three structures located along Upton Road in Duplain Township, Michigan, and was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 2015."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DUPLAIN TOWNSHIP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Duplain Township is a township in Michigan where the Grist Mill Bridge, Dam and Mill Site is located, first settled in the 1830s."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ELSIE, MICHIGAN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Elsie, Michigan is a town located approximately one-half mile west of the Grist Mill Bridge, Dam and Mill Site."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ROBERT E. CRAVEN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Robert E. Craven, likely with his brothers Joseph and Thomas, constructed a saw-mill at the Grist Mill Bridge, Dam and Mill Site in 1845."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KELLOGG BROS. AND JOHNSON&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Kellogg Bros. and Johnson purchased the Grist Mill Bridge, Dam and Mill Site in 1865."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NATIONAL REGISTER OF HISTORIC PLACES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The Grist Mill Covered Bridge was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1974."&lt;SEP&gt;"The National Register of Historic Places is the official list of the United States' historic places worthy of preservation."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;UPTON ROAD&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Upton Road is a road along which the Grist Mill Bridge, Dam and Mill Site is located."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ISLAND ROAD&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Island Road is a road near the Grist Mill Bridge, Dam and Mill Site."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MAPLE RIVER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Maple River is a river near the Grist Mill Bridge, Dam and Mill Site."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICHIGAN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Michigan is the state where the Grist Mill Bridge, Dam and Mill Site is located."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ROBERT E. CRAVEN (BROTHERS JOSEPH AND THOMAS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Joseph and Thomas Craven are Robert E. Craven's brothers who helped construct the saw-mill at the Grist Mill Bridge, Dam and Mill Site in 1845."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SAW-MILL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A saw-mill was constructed at the Grist Mill Bridge, Dam and Mill Site in 1845 and operated for nearly 20 years."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CANYON ROAD&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Canyon Road is a road that the Grist Mill Covered Bridge carries across the Brewster River in Cambridge, Vermont."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BREWSTER RIVER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Brewster River is a river that the Grist Mill Covered Bridge crosses in Cambridge, Vermont."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CAMBRIDGE, VERMONT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cambridge, Vermont is a town where the Grist Mill Covered Bridge is located, south of the village of Jeffersonville."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SCOTT COVERED BRIDGE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Scott Covered Bridge is another name for the Grist Mill Covered Bridge, located in Cambridge, Vermont."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BURR ARCH TRUSS BRIDGES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The Grist Mill Covered Bridge is a surviving example of a Burr arch truss bridge in Vermont."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CANYON&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Canyon is a feature near the Grist Mill Covered Bridge in Cambridge, Vermont."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;JEFFERSONVILLE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Jeffersonville is a village located south of the Grist Mill Covered Bridge in Cambridge, Vermont."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THOMPSON-NEELY GRIST MILL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Thompson-Neely Grist Mill is a historic grist mill located across River Road from the Thompson-Neely House and Farmstead in the upper section of the park."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GRIST MILL COVERED BRIDGE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Grist Mill Covered Bridge, also known as the Scott Covered Bridge, the Bryant Covered Bridge, and the Canyon Covered Bridge, is a historic covered bridge located in Cambridge, Vermont, carrying Canyon Road across the Brewster River."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;1830S&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TIME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The 1830s is the period when Duplain Township was first settled."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;1845&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TIME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"1845 is the year when Robert E. Craven and his brothers constructed a saw-mill at the Grist Mill Bridge, Dam and Mill Site."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;1865&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TIME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"1865 is the year when Kellogg Bros. and Johnson purchased the Grist Mill Bridge, Dam and Mill Site."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;1885&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TIME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"1885 is the year when the deck of the Grist Mill Covered Bridge was replaced."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;1792&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TIME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"1792 is the year when documentary references to bridges at or near the Grist Mill Covered Bridge location were made."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;1822&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TIME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"1822 is the year when documentary references to bridges at or near the Grist Mill Covered Bridge location were made."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;1974&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TIME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"1974 is the year when the Grist Mill Covered Bridge was listed on the National Register of Historic Places."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;1990S&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TIME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The 1990s is the decade mentioned in relation to ongoing renovations at the Thompson-Neely Grist Mill."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CANAAN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Canaan is a location mentioned in relation to the Grist Mill Covered Bridge, though not specified further in the text."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RIVER ROAD&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"River Road is a road mentioned in relation to the Thompson-Neely Grist Mill, located across from the Thompson-Neely House and Farmstead."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THOMPSON-NEELY HOUSE AND FARMSTEAD&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Thompson-Neely House and Farmstead is a historic house and farmstead located in the upper section of the park, across River Road from the Thompson-Neely Grist Mill."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THOMPSON-NEELY GRIST MILL ONGOING RENOVATIONS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Ongoing renovations are affecting the capacity and operations of the Thompson-Neely Grist Mill."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0459-cd4f437f7154</data>
    </node>
  </graph>
</graphml>
